WEBVTT

00:00:00.612 --> 00:00:18.724
<v Casey>Morning, good to have you. Thursday's the day we dig into governance, title and treaty. Today we've got Bill C-39 and the rally coverage, plus Kebaowek's fight over a nuclear waste project at Chalk River. And the big question: when a federal designation runs against a Nation's no, who actually decides? Stay with us.

00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:51.303
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Thursday, November 19th, 2026. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation. Here in the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:52.303 --> 00:01:18.095
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today we're looking at how Indigenous nations are using their own authority over land, data, and projects. Out at Horsefly Lake it's foggy and minus 5 this morning, with a high of 1 today and no precipitation expected. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is minus 6 under overcast, Williams Lake is minus 5 in fog, Quesnel is minus 4 in fog, and Alexis Creek is minus 6 and mainly clear.

00:01:18.295 --> 00:01:19.335
<v Casey>Right, on to the news.

00:01:20.735 --> 00:01:29.703
<v Riley>Most of today's news is close to home, and a lot of it is Indigenous-led work sitting next to hard questions about who holds the last word on land.

00:01:31.415 --> 00:02:06.973
<v Riley>Williams Lake First Nation closed its local Addictions Awareness Week on November 6th with a 24-foot mural called River of Recovery. It shows the Chilcotin River at Farwell Canyon, and community members added images of healing, recovery, and remembrance at the Elizabeth Grouse Gymnasium. The youngest contributor was about five and a half months old. Several elders in their 80s added to it too. The mural now hangs at the Nation's health station, and the programming ran ahead of National Addictions Awareness Week, which runs November 15th to 21st across Canada.

00:02:07.423 --> 00:02:12.687
<v Casey>A footprint from a baby and brushwork from elders, on one canvas. That's the detail that sticks.

00:02:14.447 --> 00:02:39.295
<v Casey>The BC Wildfire Service has planned a prescribed burn about 10 kilometres west of Williams Lake, on the north side of Highway 20. It's a partnership with the Williams Lake Community Forest and Williams Lake First Nation, and the aim is to reduce how severe future wildfires get near the community. The service says fire is a natural process in many of B.C.'s ecosystems, and prescribed burns are one way land managers cut fuel.

00:02:40.777 --> 00:03:09.665
<v Riley>The Tŝilhqot'in National Government has congratulated Tŝilhqot'in ʔEten Nadiltil, or TEN, on a business expansion. TEN is an Indigenous-led civil construction company doing infrastructure work across B.C. It is 51 percent owned by Dandzen Development LP, the economic development arm of the Tŝilhqot'in National Government and the six Tŝilhqot'in communities. Tribal Chief Otis Guichon Senior called the expansion a huge step in charting their own path in economic development.

00:03:10.114 --> 00:03:16.906
<v Casey>Owned by the Nation and working across the province. That's a different kind of contractor from the ones we usually read about.

00:03:18.627 --> 00:03:57.705
<v Casey>On November 12th, more than 65 rallies took place across Canada against Bill C-39, the federal Build Canada Strong Act. In Surrey, activists gathered outside the office of MP Sukh Dhaliwal. The bill would let Cabinet designate regions of "national interest" and set a one-year timeline for decisions on designated projects. Protesters and Indigenous groups say it threatens treaty rights and environmental safeguards. The federal government says it will speed projects while keeping Indigenous consultation. A House committee is studying the bill, and no date for the final House vote has been announced. The Williams Lake Tribune and rabble.ca both covered the rallies.

00:03:59.111 --> 00:04:25.535
<v Riley>Eighteen years of data collected by four First Nations shows that B.C.'s Rockfish Conservation Areas have delivered benefits as far as 160 kilometres from the protected zones. The Tyee reports that the study's lead author, Kyle Wilson of Simon Fraser University, says measuring success is hard. Rockfish species differ in diet and life cycle, and the original policy never set clear goals for researchers to measure against.

00:04:25.985 --> 00:04:30.801
<v Casey>Eighteen years of Nation-run monitoring, and the policy never said what success looked like.

00:04:32.561 --> 00:04:49.889
<v Casey>APTN News reports on a conference that examined how to get police, courts, and government to respect the laws First Nations are passing to protect their communities. The headline asks who will enforce those laws, and the conference was about making the people with badges and courtrooms recognize them in the first place.

00:04:51.423 --> 00:05:11.231
<v Riley>APTN News also reports a finding that 70 percent of Inuit children live in households that skip meals or eat less to make ends meet. Since March 2025, Inuit children aged two to five have had lower hemoglobin levels and a higher likelihood of anemia than during the period when the earlier HFV program was operating.

00:05:11.681 --> 00:05:15.737
<v Casey>Seventy percent. That number needs a program behind it, not just a headline.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:52.988
<v Casey>About 20 members of Brazil's isolated Awá people approached a government post near Tawari village in Maranhão on November 16th. They asked for food and then went back into the forest. Brazil's Indigenous affairs agency, Funai, says it is reviewing the video and accounts, has activated a contingency plan, and is keeping to its non-contact policy. Advocates point to illegal logging, hunting, and ranching pressure on the group's territory. Popular Science reports that an estimated 300 Awá remain, with advocates putting about a third in complete isolation. Al Jazeera and Newsday both covered the encounter.

00:05:54.476 --> 00:06:11.644
<v Riley>Country Life reports that Richard Whitcomb and Amie Dow of the Bute Community Land Company were named countryside champions by the Prince of Wales in his guest-edited issue. Their work is the stewardship of a Scottish forest, and the magazine ran their interview with Julie Harding.

00:06:13.044 --> 00:06:48.938
<v Casey>A draft text for COP31, the UN climate talks in Antalya, Türkiye, was released on November 16th. It proposes at least $120 billion a year in adaptation finance for developing countries by 2035, alongside faster action to phase out fossil fuels. The draft is still under negotiation and is not an agreed position. The talks are scheduled to wrap up on November 20th. The UN Environment Programme estimates developing countries will need $305 billion a year for adaptation by 2035. Ghana's environment minister has also called for money to restore land and rivers damaged by mining.

00:06:49.388 --> 00:06:54.148
<v Riley>Two numbers, $120 billion against $305 billion. The gap is the story.

00:06:55.859 --> 00:07:21.051
<v Riley>Yale Environment 360 reports that a powerful El Niño is worsening drought in several food-producing regions. The World Food Programme warns it could push 49 million more people into acute food insecurity by the end of 2027, bringing the total to 274 million. Drought has cut rainfall in northern Sudan to less than half of normal, and El Niño is forecast to peak in December.

00:07:22.521 --> 00:07:52.047
<v Casey>On November 6th, the U.S. Treasury Department sanctioned eight money-services businesses in Ontario and British Columbia. It alleges they moved funds for Iran, including by converting Iranian rials and other currencies into digital assets. The action was coordinated with FINTRAC, Canada's financial intelligence agency. U.S. officials also said several of the firms handled money for Ryan Wedding, a Canadian former Olympic snowboarder described as an alleged drug trafficker, and three firms were accused of laundering drug proceeds.

00:07:53.593 --> 00:08:02.777
<v Riley>The Tyee profiles artist Ayana V. Jackson and her Afrofuturist work, which treats the Atlantic as a place of ancestral resilience, not only historical trauma.

00:08:04.253 --> 00:08:14.741
<v Casey>RealAg Radio's Haney wrapped his series on the 2026 class of the Canadian Agricultural Hall of Fame with inductee Gary Bowers, who talks about ag advocacy and dairy genetics.

00:08:16.325 --> 00:09:09.963
<v Riley>Five U.S. agencies warned on November 5th that hackers are targeting industrial refrigeration and cold-chain monitoring systems worldwide. Those controllers run grocery stores, hospitals, food producers, and transport operators. An attacker could change temperatures or defrost settings, or hide the readings from operators, which can spoil stock and compromise storage safety. The warning followed attacks on European supermarkets on November 2nd and an incident that altered refrigeration settings at three French blood-service facilities. Investigators are also examining August refrigeration malfunctions that affected at least six military commissaries. The agencies' advice is plain: cut unneeded internet access, update firmware, and separate control systems from business networks. That's the one to end on, because the fix is dull and mostly within reach.

00:09:10.413 --> 00:09:17.669
<v Casey>Who holds the final say over what runs the system, and who gets the call when it breaks. We'll come back to that question shortly.

00:09:19.069 --> 00:09:40.629
<v Riley>The Quesnel Tillicum Society runs the Native Friendship Centre in Quesnel, and it has been doing that work since 1972. Its programs cover drug and alcohol support, family services, youth outreach, and cultural programming, and every year there's the Pow Wow. If you want to see what they offer, the website is quesnelfriendshipcentre.com.

00:09:41.181 --> 00:09:46.325
<v Casey>More than fifty years in one town is a track record anyone can check, and this one holds up.

00:09:46.425 --> 00:09:51.209
<v Casey>Right, the question of who holds the final say, and we're going to spend the next stretch on it.

00:09:52.609 --> 00:10:02.217
<v Riley>Take the C-39 rally story from earlier. My position is that the speed isn't the problem. The problem is whether a Nation can say no inside that one-year clock.

00:10:02.806 --> 00:10:22.910
<v Casey>Then ask what the bill doesn't answer. When a fast-track law and the courts decide how Indigenous consent gets counted, does consultation give a Nation a real decision over its land, or only a seat in the room? The Council of Canadians, which opposes the bill, reads it as "everything is on the menu." Cabinet holds the pen on what counts as national interest.

00:10:23.439 --> 00:10:43.095
<v Riley>The government says consultation stays inside the impact assessment, and that projects get built faster without lowering standards. The Narwhal reports that C-39 folds Indigenous consultation into that assessment, coordinated across every related federal permit. So a Nation's input goes through one door instead of many.

00:10:43.423 --> 00:10:47.719
<v Casey>One door works fine if the Nation holds the key. Who holds it under C-39?

00:10:48.045 --> 00:10:58.389
<v Riley>The ministers do, and they still have to weigh what they hear. That's the duty. A Nation that wants a project can use the clock to set conditions and take a share of the work.

00:10:58.977 --> 00:11:10.937
<v Casey>If the terms let it. The Assembly of First Nations says the bill puts commercial timelines ahead of free, prior and informed consent. A timeline a Nation can use is not the same as a timeline a Nation can refuse.

00:11:11.615 --> 00:11:22.807
<v Riley>Then write the refusal in. Co-design, equity stakes, a formal right to say no before a designation is signed. Those are drafting choices, and they cost less before approval than after.

00:11:23.352 --> 00:11:31.728
<v Casey>Cheaper for who? Co-design needs staff, legal time, and a Nation with the capacity to sit at the table. Nobody budgets for that in the press release.

00:11:32.254 --> 00:11:38.670
<v Riley>The cost is real. A no that arrives after approval is a complaint, though, and a complaint doesn't stop a backhoe.

00:11:39.238 --> 00:11:46.198
<v Casey>So who pays the people who can say no in time? A Nation's lawyers and technical staff are a line item the bill never mentions.

00:11:46.728 --> 00:12:02.568
<v Riley>Nobody has priced it. Still, the Dipping Road prescribed burn we mentioned up top is a working example of the design I'm describing. The Nation sits at the planning table with the Community Forest and the BC Wildfire Service, not reading about the burn afterward.

00:12:03.201 --> 00:12:18.985
<v Casey>That's a small project on land with a clear purpose. Fuel reduction near a community is a shared interest. A federal designation over a mine or a pipeline is not. Put the same partnership model in front of a project nobody in the town wants, and the release is a different document.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:26.420
<v Riley>Which is the test. Partnership works when the Nation wants the outcome too. The bill has to work when it doesn't.

00:12:26.722 --> 00:12:29.994
<v Casey>And C-39 doesn't say what happens then. That's the gap.

00:12:30.519 --> 00:13:02.677
<v Riley>Kebaowek is where that gets tested in court. The First Nation intends to ask the Supreme Court of Canada to hear its case against Canadian Nuclear Laboratories over a waste-disposal project at Chalk River. The Federal Court of Appeal reversed the lower court on October 16th. The lower court had said the free, prior and informed consent principle should inform federal consultation. Law-firm readings of the appeal ruling say federal regulators are not legally required to apply UNDRIP in consultation.

00:13:03.244 --> 00:13:19.220
<v Casey>A nuclear waste dump sitting inside a fight over the declaration. Those are two different questions in one case. The dump is a technology question. Whether the declaration binds a federal agency is a legal-status question, and that one reaches every Nation with a project in its territory.

00:13:19.734 --> 00:13:31.342
<v Riley>Then separate them, and the legal one is the clearer. A Supreme Court answer tells developers and Nations which process they're in. Right now they are guessing, and guessing costs more than a rule does.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:49.802
<v Casey>Guessing is cheap for the proponent. Kebaowek is paying for the application and waiting on a leave decision. A court case runs on a Nation's own money and on years of waiting. C-39 sets a one-year decision clock on designated projects. They are on different clocks, and whoever has the bigger budget sets the pace.

00:13:50.436 --> 00:14:05.348
<v Riley>Different clocks, same question. Kebaowek also has leave to intervene in the separate Gitxaala appeal, and that appeal is likely to shape the wider legal status of UNDRIP. Whatever gets decided there reaches a Cabinet designation as well as a courtroom.

00:14:05.913 --> 00:14:10.681
<v Casey>Likely. Not decided. And the Supreme Court hasn't said whether it will hear Kebaowek at all.

00:14:11.208 --> 00:14:16.160
<v Riley>A leave decision is still a yes or a no, and a Nation can plan around either one.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:21.776
<v Casey>A no is a decision too. Ask a Nation that spent years on a case how it plans around a no.

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:27.766
<v Riley>I don't have a good answer to that one. The planning isn't what I'd defend. The clarity is.

00:14:28.334 --> 00:14:52.934
<v Casey>Then we agree the clarity is what's missing. Now the U.S. version, which runs on the same logic. The Bipartisan American Affordability and Jobs Act would speed permitting for major projects. In October, nine tribal organizations, including the Native American Rights Fund, wrote to Senate leadership asking that provisions weakening consultation and protection for sacred places be removed. The Senate advanced it, as we covered last Friday.

00:14:53.583 --> 00:15:09.887
<v Riley>They asked, and the bill moved forward. The mechanics matter here. Hyperallergic says the bill narrows Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act. That's the review requiring agencies to weigh effects on historic properties, often with tribal consultation.

00:15:10.418 --> 00:15:24.570
<v Casey>Often with consultation. The bill takes tribal nations out of much of that review. Some tribal leaders have called it the single greatest threat to sacred tribal lands. That's a strong claim, and the bill's language is where it gets tested, not the quote.

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:37.592
<v Riley>Section 106 was never a veto, though. Agencies weigh effects and consult, and they don't have to agree. A consultation step with no teeth is a weak tool. The fix is to give it teeth, not to delete the step.

00:15:38.253 --> 00:15:48.885
<v Casey>Teeth would be nice. The bill you're describing hasn't been written. The one on the table takes the step out of much of the review, and the people who would have used it were not kept in the drafting.

00:15:49.501 --> 00:15:57.005
<v Riley>The tribal letter asks for those provisions to come out, which means the text can still move. That's a fact, not a promise.

00:15:57.358 --> 00:16:00.462
<v Casey>It can still move. Who decides that is the part I'd watch.

00:16:01.062 --> 00:16:20.870
<v Riley>Now the other design. On November 18th, Natan Obed, president of Inuit Tapiriit Kanatami, and Indigenous Services Minister Mandy Gull-Masty announced the Pirutsiagutiit National Framework. It sets direction for Canada, Inuit Treaty Organizations, and partners on how an Inuit-led program for Inuit children gets implemented.

00:16:21.447 --> 00:16:35.711
<v Casey>The government release is the only source on it, so I'll keep to its wording. The part I'd copy is the order. The Inuit organizations set the direction, and Ottawa comes in as a partner. The part to check is who holds the money and the accountability terms.

00:16:36.344 --> 00:16:47.024
<v Riley>On the design, we agree. Direction set first is exactly what the first two beats were missing. A Nation that sets the terms at the start doesn't need a court to win the argument later.

00:16:47.618 --> 00:16:56.490
<v Casey>It doesn't need a court, but it does need a funding line that lasts past the announcement. Frameworks die in the budget cycle more often than in the courts.

00:16:57.149 --> 00:17:06.853
<v Riley>I can't argue that from the release. What I can say is that the order matters. Consent built in at the start is cheaper to hold than consent demanded at the end.

00:17:07.183 --> 00:17:10.367
<v Casey>Cheaper if it survives the first budget. That's the test.

00:17:10.939 --> 00:17:31.891
<v Riley>Which brings it home. The Supreme Court of Canada's 2014 Tŝilhqot'in decision declared Aboriginal title for the first time in Canada. And TEN, the Nation-owned contractor from the roundup, is 51 percent held by Dandzen Development LP. A Nation that owns the contractor is the one building, not just being notified about the build.

00:17:32.555 --> 00:17:50.891
<v Casey>Owning the contractor gets a Nation a seat at the bid table. It doesn't give a Nation a no once a Cabinet has designated a region. Ownership is one question. A veto is another. C-39's text is where the second one would be settled, and I haven't seen anything in it that says a title-holder can stop a designated project.

00:17:51.554 --> 00:18:06.706
<v Riley>Agreed that the designation is the test. Still, a Nation that does the work has standing from the first meeting, not the last. A title-holder with a construction company at the table is in a different position than a title-holder with a letter in the mail.

00:18:07.355 --> 00:18:18.947
<v Casey>Doing the work is a different job from stopping it. Owning a contractor gets you work. Stopping a project needs a legal right, and title on paper and a statute that overrides it in practice are not the same.

00:18:19.604 --> 00:18:31.044
<v Riley>Then the question to put to the Tŝilhqot'in National Government is simple. If C-39 passes as written, does a designation over title land require the Nation's consent, or only its consultation?

00:18:31.348 --> 00:18:34.996
<v Casey>That's the question for Ottawa too. And the rally coverage doesn't answer it.

00:18:35.649 --> 00:18:42.361
<v Riley>So where should a Nation's no live? In a statute, in a court ruling, or in structures a Nation owns and runs?

00:18:42.678 --> 00:18:50.902
<v Casey>The place that survives a change of government. Nobody has answered that yet. And the Cabinet holding the pen is the same one that can designate the region.

00:18:51.488 --> 00:19:05.816
<v Riley>Then the open question is who decides when a designation overrides a Nation's no, and whether the Nation hears about it before the designation is signed, or after. Structures like Pirutsiagutiit and TEN show what it looks like when the Nation sets terms first.

00:19:06.363 --> 00:19:18.083
<v Casey>After is the default in every version I've read. I'm not signing off on the bill until I've read the designation test in writing. If you want to send a story tip, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:18.355 --> 00:19:20.675
<v Riley>Read the clause first. Have a good Thursday.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:31.564
<v Casey>We haven't actually talked with anyone from the First Nations communications side for today's episode, and that's a conversation we'd really welcome, so if that's you, reach out to us.

00:19:32.164 --> 00:19:51.292
